Q: Warning Message: preg_match_all() expects parameter 2 to be string, array given A: <ul>
<li>Please, first, understand that profanity redaction is trivially circumvented. Nefarious humans are <strong>going</strong> to find a way to sidestep your patterns if they have the determination. Those word boundaries will not match if a user simply prepends or appends an underscore to their profane term.</li>
<li>It would be a very bad idea to sanitize &quot;on read&quot; - that is particularly costly if you fetch all rows from your database table. Your processes should sanitize &quot;on write&quot; for performance and sanity reasons. Simply do not allow the profanity to be added to your database.</li>
<li>Because a <code>sanitizeProfanity()</code> function has no real context or business logic, it would be a good candidate for a helper instead of a private/protected method in the Forum_model. It is entirely possible that another model or layer might want to leverage that functionality. I'll keep my refactored sanitizing method in your model for simplicity of posting.</li>
<li>Your sanitization process intends to deterge string values, but if you pass a query result (2d array from <code>result()</code>) to it, of course, PHP will choke. You need to isolate the specific column value from a given row and run your process on that string.</li>
<li>In this situation, <code>preg_match_all()</code> followed by <code>preg_replace()</code> should be refactored as a <code>preg_replace_callback()</code> call. Notice how clean the method becomes as a result of consolidating patterns and omitting loops.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class Forum_model extends CI_Model
{
    // ...

    private function sanitizeProfanity(string $text): string
    {
        $filter_terms = [
            '\bass(es|holes?)?\b',
            '\bshit(e|ted|ting|ty|head)\b',
            '\bf[ ]*u[ ]*c[ ]*k[]*\b',
            '\bf+u+c+k+\b',
        ];
        return preg_replace_callback(
            '/' . implode('|', $filter_terms) . '/i',
            fn($match) =&gt; str_repeat('*', strlen($match[0])),
            $text
        );
    }

    // ...

    public function createPost(string $title, string $description): int
    {
        $row['title'] = $this-&gt;sanitizeProfanity($title);
        $row['slug'] = strtolower(url_title($row['title']));
        $row['description'] = $this-&gt;sanitizeProfanity($description);
        // created_at should be DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP in the database
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;insert('forums', $row);
        return $this-&gt;db-&gt;insert_id();
    }

}
</code></pre>

Q: Kotlin/Native/cinterop pointer to custom class A: <p>I'll put the comments together in one answer to make it clearer. I immediately apologize for my English.</p>
<p>In Kotlin/Native, you cannot declare var with fields in classes that inherit from CPointed. Such classes should reflect the C‑structure in its original form, without unnecessary fields.Otherwise, the compiler will throw an error that you already know.</p>
<p>Your version of the class will not work, because x and y are Kotlin fields, not fields of the C structure, and they have nothing to do with the memory that rawPtr points to. The x and y values should be read/written from/to the memory that rawPtr is viewing, not stored in a Kotlin object.</p>
<p>Judging by your fragment with memScoped and allocPointerTo&lt;Point&gt;(), you are working with a C-structure in your own memory. Therefore, in any case, you must have a structure somewhere like</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct {    
    int x;    
    int y;
} Point;
</code></pre>
<p>then your Kotlin class should look something like this</p>
<pre><code>import kotlinx.cinterop.*

@ExternalSymbol
external class Point(rawPtr: NativePtr) : CPointed(rawPtr) {    
    val x: Int by value(offsetOf(Point::class, &quot;x&quot;)) // you can do this val x: Int by value(offsetOf&lt;Point&gt;(Point::x))    
    val y: Int by value(offsetOf(Point::class, &quot;y&quot;))
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here, x and y are not class fields, but delegates that read/write to memory at the required offsets. This is the correct representation of the C‑structure. In your case, this line allocPointerTo&lt;Point&gt; calls the dot type created by cinterop for the C structure.</p>
<p>@ExternalSymbol - is an annotation that marks classes that have an external definition in C/C++ code.<br />
value - for accessing structure fields via offsets</p>
<p>offsetOf - calculates the field offset in the structure</p>
<p>You can use it either in the version that you have written, or</p>
<pre><code>memScoped {    
    val testPtr = alloc&lt;Point&gt;().apply {        
        pointed.x = 123        
        pointed.y = 321    
    }

    val xValue = testPtr.pointed.x    
    val yValue = testPtr.pointed.y
}
</code></pre>

Q: Typescript if conditional before returning a new object A: <p>I would probably add a return type to the UserService.search method. That way you'll have code completion and the compiler will be happy.</p>
<pre><code>interface User {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    type: string;
    password: string;
}
class UserService {
    async search(url: string): Promise&lt;User[]&gt; {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        return response.json();
    }
}

class Foo {
    constructor(private UserService: UserService) {}

    async bar() {
        const users = await this.UserService.search(&quot;/users&quot;);
        return users.map((user) =&gt; {
            if (!user.name) {
                // error...
            }
        })
    }
}
</code></pre>
